Can scantwopermhk() run on cluster mode? The answer is probably a "No", because I didn't see n.cluster option.
Can I run scantwopermhk() in smaller batches then combine the results with a c() like scantwo permutations?
Currently the scantwo permutation is too slow.

On Thursday, January 29, 2015 at 12:46:00 PM UTC-5, Karl Broman wrote:

> Memory is probably the limiting factor, so I would watch the memory usage and use only so many cores that the total memory usage stays below what's available.
>
> karl
>
> On Jan 29, 2015, at 10:04 AM, Gill <ygu...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> > Thanks,  I will switch to "hk" then. when I use 4 nodes and 4 ppns, should I use n.cluster=4?
> >
> > On Thursday, January 29, 2015 at 10:44:54 AM UTC-5, Karl Broman wrote:
> > Scantwo permutations take a long time, especially if you have many markers.
> >
> > 1000 permutations will take at least as 1000x as long as running scantwo once, and there can problems with running out of memory which will really slow things down.
> >
> > If you can use method="hk", things will be much faster.
> >
> > Also try the scantwopermhk() function, which works only for Haley-Knott regression, but was reorganized to try to speed things up.
> >
> > I'd also recommend doing the permutations in smaller batches and then combining the results with c().
> >
> > karl
> >
> > On Jan 29, 2015, at 8:26 AM, Gill <ygu...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > > Hi,
> > > I'm recently running a qtl analysis on cluster, but the permutation took too long! After running for 4 days Iam still waiting for the permutation results? Am I doing something wrong?
